---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="delete-account">
          <p class="dx-lede">
            You can delete your Vector account yourself, from the account page. It cannot be undone, so Vector asks you
            to type your own email address first.
          </p>

          <h2>How to do it</h2>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><span /><div><strong>Open your account</strong><p>Go to <a href="/account">vectordev.ai/account</a> and sign in.</p></div></li>
            <li><span /><div><strong>Scroll to Account</strong><p>The last section on the page, under Sign out, has <strong>Delete account…</strong>.</p></div></li>
            <li><span /><div><strong>Type your email to confirm</strong><p>The delete button stays disabled until the address you type matches the one on the account.</p></div></li>
          </ol>

          <h2>What deletion does</h2>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>Your sign-in</dt><dd>The account and its email and name are deleted. You can create a new account with the same address afterwards; it will not be the same account, and nothing is restored to it.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your subscription</dt><dd>An active subscription is cancelled so nothing charges again, and your customer record at Stripe — the name and email it holds — is deleted. Stripe keeps the underlying invoices for tax and accounting, no longer attached to a person.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your licence key</dt><dd>Revoked. A desktop app activated with it stops passing activation.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>CLI tokens</dt><dd>Revoked, so a terminal already signed in with <code>vector login</code> stops verifying. Without this they would keep working until they expired, up to ninety days later.</dd></div>
          </dl>

          <h2>What deletion does not touch</h2>
          <p>
            Your repositories, your sessions and Vector's memory live on your own machine, and deleting the account does
            not reach them. Remove them yourself: sessions and memory are in Vector's data directory (<code>vector db
            path</code> prints where the database is), and <code>vector uninstall</code> removes the CLI and its files.
            Anything you published to your own Vercel, Netlify or Supabase account belongs to those accounts and stays
            there.
          </p>

          <p class="note">
            If the account page reports that deletion is not configured, nothing was changed. Write to support and it
            will be done by hand.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
